  "metadata": {
    "title": "Qualcomm Nears Deal for AI Chip Startup Modular",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "(Bloomberg) -- Qualcomm Inc. is in advanced talks to acquire Modular Inc. in a transaction valuing the artificial intelligence chip startup at about $4 billion, according to people familiar with the matter.\nMost Read from Bloomberg\n-\nUS and Iran Make 'Progress' in Talks, Aim to Keep Hormuz Open\n-\nStocks Fall as SpaceX's Rout Offsets US-Iran Hopes: Markets Wrap\n-\nMeta Taps New WhatsApp Boss as Part of $900 Million Investment\n-\nIranian Negotiators Said to Still Be Engaged in Talks With US\nA transaction could be announced as soon as the coming weeks, said the people, who asked not to be identified because the information is private.\nA final agreement hasn't been reached and the talks could still end without one or the details could change, the people added. A spokesperson for Qualcomm declined to comment. A representative for Modular couldn't immediately be reached.\nChipmakers of all sizes are looking to seize on technology and talent at the cutting edge of AI. Qualcomm is trying to pad out its product line up and forge alliances that will make it a competitor to market leader Nvidia Corp.\nModular was founded in 2022 in Silicon Valley by Chris Lattner and Tim Davis, who met at Google and were \"frustrated by AI's fragmented infrastructure,\" according to its website.\nThe company raised $250 million at a $1.6 billion valuation in September 2025, bringing its total capital raised to $380 million.\nModular is backed by investors including DFJ Growth, Factory, General Catalyst, Google Ventures, Greylock Partners and US Innovative Technology Fund.\nThe rapidly evolving landscape for so-called inferencing chips is also complicating — and in some cases boosting — valuations for startups like Modular. Deals including Nvidia's reported $20 billion licensing agreement to acquire assets from Groq Inc. and a new funding round for Intel Capital-backed SambaNova Systems Inc. have prompted players to reevaluate their strategic value to deep-pocketed incumbents in the AI ecosystem.\nQualcomm has been pursuing tuck-in acquisitions in recent years after its deal for NXP Semiconductors NV was scrapped after not receiving regulatory approval. It agreed to buy London-listed semiconductor company Alphawave IP Group Plc last year for about $2.4 billion in cash.\n--With assistance from Natasha Mascarenhas and Ian King.\nMost Read from Bloomberg Businessweek\n©2026 Bloomberg L.P.",
